#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\n&=4.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd25587-a492-5f8f-84f4-e747217aa1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286504} {:answer/id #uuid "749265ec-a632-578c-ba48-869c9af399ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286505} {:answer/id #uuid "a650c7fe-4f48-528b-8b34-de554a108c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286506} {:answer/id #uuid "3b14a418-103a-5fb6-8f04-63ddc02daabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286507} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4783dc-8d72-5371-819c-07486a86cbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "749265ec-a632-578c-ba48-869c9af399ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286505} :answer-field/id #uuid "88dad7ce-ab8f-53b2-ae30-acd9f22d68bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81f753e1-569a-5d13-ba2f-798190f0ed00" :question/math-academy-id "q-114245" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[2x-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19380428-ddeb-5bea-a1fd-1060af3fee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296219} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1ce76-ddf9-5c3c-997b-fe9820824bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296220} {:answer/id #uuid "03f0a35e-f062-5c43-8839-ca60a8ac9986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296221} {:answer/id #uuid "e268eb65-6b1a-5013-9363-201fca83ae3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296222} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b4f08e-5ce6-5522-9abe-760276d1d95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b4f08e-5ce6-5522-9abe-760276d1d95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296223} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ceb23dd-97df-5bf4-817d-999f2b55060d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4290f82-8e72-5fbe-9469-7463c9848c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114252" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x-2){e}^{-x}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0-2){e}^{0}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+4}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c0e80d-921b-58bc-804d-12b71eedbd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296226} {:answer/id #uuid "9c59c1b0-f3e1-5a69-85d9-c373d2fee880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296227} {:answer/id #uuid "91494f9c-3745-548b-a479-ef8d566f6780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296228} {:answer/id #uuid "14394eea-a463-5999-939d-74a3553602fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296229} {:answer/id #uuid "959277e5-7870-5f8a-a16e-d87783bc3591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91494f9c-3745-548b-a479-ef8d566f6780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296228} :answer-field/id #uuid "5846e823-f41f-53ec-bcf4-f17ea85273bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2973b5dc-b98a-5c49-9369-5168692b0047" :question/math-academy-id "q-115517" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[x]^{′}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b84403db-da4b-5787-89e9-275036d45a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296233} {:answer/id #uuid "f9156461-004d-5c81-9b21-9e5b9d232dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296234} {:answer/id #uuid "ab09c9e8-36a1-52b8-b879-96817ab624a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296235} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a4cb9-af44-593a-bbcb-7977966f8966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296236} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9c286d-41ea-54d9-83bc-2e49ccf798f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a4cb9-af44-593a-bbcb-7977966f8966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296236} :answer-field/id #uuid "f93e74ed-769b-5257-a7cc-fb02c0a121c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e65e1c0-3689-5fde-9198-3623eacf6539" :question/math-academy-id "q-115523" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]^{′}}{{[{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4617dc05-b7c3-54a3-8ba8-402846b15bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa083fa1-257f-52c1-8bdd-4e16083cfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5df6a8-9107-5e7c-a593-62f2641b4f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa7d81-a547-5b0b-86d2-58082d6e4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296243} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} :answer-field/id #uuid "11cbad53-4033-52c7-8499-d026641bafc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54e2735-245d-5798-9c48-ef40ec1547e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b125944-1856-5ab1-beb7-434241d41c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264186} {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "2c451fa2-fc51-5189-9cb0-6f4c159f8621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264188} {:answer/id #uuid "619df33a-7843-5c74-9da5-34186caf63b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264189} {:answer/id #uuid "bc33fa1a-f686-5b11-9603-3f8b71680708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad13d99-b787-5977-a513-f2b4171b3171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f71e509-23a7-5349-9c54-3e2d5f1db3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31257" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}+4x+3]^{′}}{[2x+3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f332ffd-cd76-5431-a8dd-9823d88bd64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296247} {:answer/id #uuid "2812d2d1-23b9-5f2a-99dd-10996589c99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296248} {:answer/id #uuid "c61f5cbc-5175-50dc-92ca-7ff6dc72b5df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249} {:answer/id #uuid "5688c19f-acea-5d3b-973b-cd9a4e2e907a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} {:answer/id #uuid "31d2863f-732c-5fa9-addd-50d86d59f7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5688c19f-acea-5d3b-973b-cd9a4e2e907a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} :answer-field/id #uuid "3128421f-6f94-5bf1-91ad-b2609215c5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "577968a4-86aa-5318-b90b-0d83b1516122" :question/math-academy-id "q-31271" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8]^{′}}{[x-2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{1} \\\\  & =4(2)^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073b7737-0376-5911-8179-26929be1ccd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296254} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9108a3-8bc4-5bec-a30e-71cb3a81451b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa9108a3-8bc4-5bec-a30e-71cb3a81451b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296255} :answer-field/id #uuid "700668c2-4e99-529a-ba8c-5244bb440615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d9082f5-09a8-5fab-a36c-a6d340884982" :question/math-academy-id "q-314139" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{[{x}^{3}-27]^{′}}{[x-3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295a7726-c40a-5634-b4bd-f9153064e185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264196} {:answer/id #uuid "070a9933-e030-5e90-91ee-ba93985c8446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264197} {:answer/id #uuid "96a4b77a-1bf6-5734-8a77-5f373a0b0900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264198} {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb24de-5359-5e54-a584-738f4806185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e33387-f9f3-5179-b023-40bc792c4b37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba7f82da-4dd1-526e-9dfc-73ae8a9ea727" :question/math-academy-id "q-31517" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02299284-7905-5e23-a99b-fab257fad339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275713} {:answer/id #uuid "45f7f7ff-fa85-556d-ac9b-fb6cf116a017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275714} {:answer/id #uuid "96b91ed0-23a5-5a64-b958-be50242ffb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275715} {:answer/id #uuid "19d23dce-89ec-5bf3-8b43-cb25fdebb900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} {:answer/id #uuid "29f955b3-1f9d-5ebc-9826-4d116af6cf84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d23dce-89ec-5bf3-8b43-cb25fdebb900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275716} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3289c5e-cad9-5a38-9f0d-b79099f92a16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1e8835d-2849-5c15-b2b3-5060e0821698" :question/math-academy-id "q-31585" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-x-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ffda00-8b89-5130-9819-271e37755096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a003fd-4e3a-50cc-8e19-f882d4516ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066553} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb7ebd7-3376-5bc7-97e2-421e92d39605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066554} {:answer/id #uuid "155d1ce5-aba5-58d8-b553-12e130aad8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066555} {:answer/id #uuid "40cf65d0-cc70-56c5-94d9-ac421b7f839c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffda00-8b89-5130-9819-271e37755096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066552} :answer-field/id #uuid "847a3e94-f49d-52fc-837e-6bc89d115615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71ad4f56-be44-5504-8dcb-a974b170b18f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31597" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d483070b-321e-52c4-9503-66f96824a418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296258} {:answer/id #uuid "1cef9843-1327-529c-8122-51addf761c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d483070b-321e-52c4-9503-66f96824a418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296258} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0e3ee9-a364-50d4-9c4e-1c649c388b05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "335861dc-0c2a-5fbe-8432-8398a3d7dcd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336782" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}{2(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}^{2}}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot 4{sin}^{2}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{4{cos}^{3}⁡(x)\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}}{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}2{cos}^{3}⁡(x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{3}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ee1e745-ecc0-5582-9777-1be0d16ee7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee1e745-ecc0-5582-9777-1be0d16ee7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296262} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f0e48d-0ce7-5543-ae60-5debaebe5293" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e728eba-52e7-5b88-ae92-5b6b07f0dcea" :question/math-academy-id "q-336787" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}]^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebe3ac8d-a0cd-5920-8bb8-598a46e5d794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296265} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3eb583-b5b8-59df-a295-060954a5b36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3eb583-b5b8-59df-a295-060954a5b36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296266} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdc83a8d-2600-5334-b8c5-80bdb79fd750" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3435a77-b21b-58be-902d-60472eebe78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336874" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11]^{′}}{[4{x}^{3}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{15{x}^{2}+12x}{12{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1)^{2}+12(1)}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b16ddda-957e-55df-a822-dc5432ee7125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b16ddda-957e-55df-a822-dc5432ee7125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296269} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3353fd1-8788-5859-b1fb-4979b19ffc98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a9c9c3f-7fbb-5a8d-b57d-d46d296a1a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-336879" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2(0)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c82f9f4-cb1e-51c7-90ff-b7bb219bd1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c82f9f4-cb1e-51c7-90ff-b7bb219bd1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296272} :answer-field/id #uuid "76a43d30-43ed-5cdf-90ad-be1e3e53597f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d129fe1-3400-5f33-9a83-b534c9ef34a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336910" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[2x]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} {:answer/id #uuid "333fcf9c-5ec8-5552-ad22-66943ec6c9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(4))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46dd4f2-d324-5542-a7e1-3429ab75b92c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e0f7884-a830-5489-a8ed-a70ba5c64f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-336932" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} {:answer/id #uuid "4919d0de-8116-5b72-91dd-335bf99cd522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cacba6-2b91-5aed-b8c1-d516321b9e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb212d02-e01a-5a05-96a9-bd2df6014b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336971" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da55b3cd-b3e8-59e9-b9e8-070e83112e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286528} {:answer/id #uuid "07087731-666d-58af-a2e7-6a0a907cf3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286529} {:answer/id #uuid "02e62621-c735-5fdf-b340-2c7fa45763c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286530} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1bb1e8-02a3-58ad-874f-067a6a2b11d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286531} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cdfcb3-2017-5148-b268-7e1b92ce6a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1bb1e8-02a3-58ad-874f-067a6a2b11d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286531} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d31bd59-f417-56ce-bfde-f80567be8b4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "841794cb-2192-56f5-9b25-7e39f616568c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72328" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[100x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100}{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f872b-6dc6-53e9-9375-a11573728d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264252} {:answer/id #uuid "d5572dd1-40b5-5472-96e5-b8731a9f950c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264253} {:answer/id #uuid "9697133b-54a1-5709-8bdc-407fdbdd8b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264254} {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} {:answer/id #uuid "99e51ad0-94fb-5a17-a2b1-81e5fa5b77bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f36a378-4860-5a2e-8c9c-2f52d95b5aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21b4d0f5-e835-5e6f-8545-1cd83c1350d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
